NOTE on latency: 1.05M cycles = 4.195 ms to move the full 1 MB language-step
weight working set. The HBM port is byte-wide (int8 element type) → ~1 byte/
cycle → ~1M cycles for 1 MB. This is BENIGN: the language path runs at 1 Hz
(1000 ms budget per language step), so a 4.195 ms weight load uses only 0.42%
of the budget — exactly the "bursty HBM traffic, bus free the rest of the
time" behaviour the memory-hierarchy claim wants. A v1 could widen the m_axi
port to 512-bit for a ~64x faster load if ever needed; not necessary for the
1 Hz language rate. Flagging human_ack(latency_above_200000) for honesty; the
number is well within the architectural budget.

--- paper §III.E memory-hierarchy note ---
HBM channel count for Table V: this controller uses 1 m_axi read port. The
paper's "4 of 32 HBM channels" figure would be reached by instantiating 4
such controllers (or one 4-port variant) across the weight working set — the
single-port controller here validates the burst-tiling mechanism.

